Q: nodeJS localstorage getting values A: <p>OP seems to have found an answer, but didn't post it.  For anyone else who needs an answer to this, I'll share one here anyway.</p>
<p>Browser <code>localStorage</code> and server-side <code>node-localstorage</code> are completely separate. OP's issue is a common misunderstanding.</p>
<p>Doing this below, in NodeJS, will only read from a file-based storage on your server, not the browser:</p>
<pre><code>var localStorage = new LocalStorage('./scratch');
console.log(localStorage.getItem('userToken'));
</code></pre>
<p>So you will need to send the token from the browser to the server, that's the correct and recommended way:</p>
<p>One way to do this correctly is by sending via request:</p>
<p>in the frontend for the browser:</p>
<pre><code>const token = localStorage.getItem('userToken');

fetch('/getitnow', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {
    'Authorization': `Bearer ${token}`
  }
});
</code></pre>
<p>in NodeJS on the backend:</p>
<pre><code>app.post('/getitnow', (req, res) =&gt; {
  const token = req.headers.authorization;
  console.log(token);
});
</code></pre>

Q: How to make Ctrl+C always quit a while loop in Bash properly? A: <p>You can kill bash process group when sigint received:</p>
<pre><code>func() {
  trap 'kill -- -$$' INT
  while ! foo bar; do :; done
}
</code></pre>
<p>You can handle all commands exit statuses:</p>
<pre><code>func() {
  while ! foo bar; do : || exit $?; done
}
</code></pre>

Q: Is there a browser-only JavaScript solution to unlock a password-protected PDF? A: <p>LibPDF did actually work. However, I ran into an issue where owner/user password restrictions prevented manipulating metadata directly. The fix was to clone all pages into a brand new PDF document instead.</p>
<p>Here's the implementation for anyone who hits the same wall:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>let unlockedBytes;
try {
    // Attempt native protection removal
    pdf.removeProtection();
    unlockedBytes = await pdf.save();
} catch (err) {
    // Fallback: clone pages into a fresh document
    // This sidesteps owner-password restrictions on metadata
    console.warn(&quot;removeProtection() failed, falling back to page cloning.&quot;, err);

    const newPdf = await PDF.create();
    const pageCount = pdf.getPageCount();
    const pageIndices = Array.from({ length: pageCount }, (_, i) =&gt; i);

    // copyPagesFrom returns the copied page objects but does NOT insert them automatically
    const copiedPages = await newPdf.copyPagesFrom(pdf, pageIndices);
    for (const page of copiedPages) {
        newPdf.addPage(page);
    }

    unlockedBytes = await newPdf.save();
}

const blob = new Blob([unlockedBytes], { type: &quot;application/pdf&quot; });
const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
</code></pre>
<p>The key fix is that <code>copyPagesFrom</code> returns the copied page objects and you have to call <code>addPage()</code> on each one explicitly. Without this the new PDF saves as blank.</p>

